	<body>
		<div id="page_0" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.883em;top:2.5524em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_09" style="word-spacing:0.0126em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 1 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:21.5682em;top:4.5533em;"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NijiLTONHxg" target="_blank"><span class="pdf24_10 pdf24_11 pdf24_12">ECG/EKG &nbsp;</span></a></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:7.379em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">Cardiac Conduction System</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">: This is the electrical current that moves through the myocardium to make it contract. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:9.032em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">Heart Muscle Cells</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_12" style="word-spacing:0.0004em;">: These cells are all connected and have a negative charge at rest. When the heart needs to &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:10.3544em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">contract, this charge changes from negative to positive, a process called depolarization. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:12.0074em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Sinoatrial Node (SA Node)</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_18" style="word-spacing:0.0013em;">: Also known as the heart's pacemaker, it sets the heart's rhythm at 60 to 100 beats per &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:13.3298em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0003em;">minute. It spontaneously goes positive and sends a wave of positive charge through the atria. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:14.9827em;"><a href="https://humanbiomedia.org/simulations/circulatory-system/cardiac-cycle/phase-sequence.html" target="_blank"><span class="pdf24_19 pdf24_11 pdf24_18" style="word-spacing:0.0009em;">Electrocardiogram (ECG/EKG)</span></a><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_20" style="word-spacing:0.001em;">: This device picks up the changes in charge. </span><a href="https://www.humanbiomedia.org/activities/circulatory-system/cardiac-cycle/phase-cycle.html" target="_blank"><span class="pdf24_19 pdf24_11 pdf24_16">(ECK/EKG) &nbsp;</span></a></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:16.6357em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">Atrioventricular Node (</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_21">A</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">V </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_17">N</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">ode)</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">: Located between the atria and ventricles (lower chambers), it pauses the signal &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:17.9581em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">slightly before sending it through specialized fibers called the bundle of His and bundle branches. These fibers then &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:19.2805em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_22" style="word-spacing:0.0007em;">send the wave of positive charges through the ventricles. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:20.9335em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Repolarization</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">: After the heart muscle depolarizes, it needs to reset to a &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:22.2559em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_22" style="word-spacing:0.0006em;">negative charge again, which is called repolarization. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:23.9089em;"><a href="https://humanbiomedia.org/simulations/circulatory-system/cardiac-cycle/interactive-display.html" target="_blank"><span class="pdf24_19 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">ECG Readings</span></a><a href="https://humanbiomedia.org/simulations/circulatory-system/cardiac-cycle/interactive-display.html" target="_blank"><span class="pdf24_23 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">: </span></a><a href="https://humanbiomedia.org/simulations/circulatory-system/cardiac-cycle/phase-analysis.html" target="_blank"><span class="pdf24_19 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">(Cardiac Cycle) </span></a><a href="https://www.humanbiomedia.org/activities/circulatory-system/cardiac-cycle/phase-cycle.html" target="_blank"><span class="pdf24_19 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">(Cardiac Cycle 2) &nbsp;</span></a></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:25.5521em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_26" style="word-spacing:0.0274em;">P Wave: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0007em;">Represents atrial depolarization. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:26.8795em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0003em;">QRS Complex: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0009em;">Represents ventricular depolarization. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:28.207em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_26" style="word-spacing:0.0276em;">T Wave</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0006em;">: Represents ventricular repolarization. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:29.5344em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">Atrial Repolarization: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">Happens at the same time as ventricular &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:30.8716em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">depolarization and is hidden behind the QRS complex on an ECG. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:32.1842em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_26" style="word-spacing:0.0274em;">U Wave: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">Represents the repolarization of the Purkinje fibers or the recovery phase of the ventricles. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:33.852em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_27" style="word-spacing:0.0049em;">In summary, the SA node sets the pace, the </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_21">A</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">V </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">n</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">ode delays the signal, and the </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">bundle of His </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">and bundle branches &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:35.1744em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">send the signal to the ventricles to contract. The ECG shows the electrical activity of the heart, including atrial &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:36.4968em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0011em;">depolarization, ventricular depolarization, and ventricular repolarization. &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_1" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.883em;top:2.5524em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_09" style="word-spacing:0.0126em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 2 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.3054em;"><span class="pdf24_28 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0em;">Strip Reading &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:8.4082em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_26" style="word-spacing:0.0276em;">P Wave</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0007em;">: Represents atrial depolarization, which leads to &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:9.5581em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">contraction of atria P wave is created when atria contract &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:11.8481em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">Duration: 0.08 to 0.10 seconds (80 to 100 ms) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:13.0024em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">2 to 2.5 small boxes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:18.0269em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">PR Interval</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">: Demonstrates time it takes for an electrical &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:19.1768em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">signal to go from the atria to </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_21">A</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">V </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">n</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16">ode. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:20.6044em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">Duration: 0.12 to 0.20 seconds (120 to 200 ms) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.7587em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0006em;">3 to 5 small boxes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:26.5769em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">PR Segment</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">: This demonstrates the delay the </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_21">A</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">V </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">n</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">ode &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:27.7268em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.001em;">created. </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_21">A</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">V </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">n</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_29" style="word-spacing:0.0025em;">ode is the gatekeeper, giving the atria time &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:28.8767em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">to dump blood into the ventricles. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:30.3043em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">Duration: 0.04 to 0.12 seconds &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:31.4586em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0006em;">1 to 3 small boxes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:36.2332em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0003em;">QRS Complex: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0006em;">It represents ventricular depolarization &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:37.3831em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0006em;">(leading to contraction of the ventricles). Atrial &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:38.533em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">repolarization also occurs here, but it is not visible &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:39.9606em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">Duration: 0.06 to 0.12 seconds (60 to 120 ms) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:41.1149em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">1.5 to 3 small boxes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:45.3833em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">QT Interval: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:46.5234em;z-index:704;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">Duration: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_30">V</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_31">a</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0006em;">ries with heart rate but generally &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:47.6875em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">0.35 to 0.44 seconds (350 to 440 ms) for males &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:48.8374em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">and 0.36 to 0.46 seconds (360 to 460 ms) for &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:49.9873em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_32" style="word-spacing:0.004em;">females. Corrected QT interval (QTc) is used to &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:51.1372em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">account for heart rate variations. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:52.2773em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_33" style="word-spacing:0.0069em;">9 to 11 small boxes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:55.8479em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">ST Segment: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:56.988em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_34" style="word-spacing:0.0029em;">Duration: Typically isoelectric (flat), with no set &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:58.1521em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">duration. It begins at the end of the QRS &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:59.302em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">complex (J point) and ends at the beginning of &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:60.4519em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">the T wave. &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_2" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.883em;top:2.5523em;"><span class="pdf24_35 pdf24_25 pdf24_36" style="word-spacing:0.0127em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 3 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.8042em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_37" style="word-spacing:0.0273em;">T Wave: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">Represents beginning of ventricular &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:5.9541em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_22" style="word-spacing:0.0006em;">repolarization (relaxation), caused by large size of the &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:7.104em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">ventricles as they relax &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:8.5316em;z-index:149;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Duration: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_38">V</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_31">a</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">riable, typically 0.10 to 0.25 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:9.6957em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">seconds (100 to 250 ms) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:10.8358em;z-index:197;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_38">V</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_31">a</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">riable, typically 2.5 to 6 small boxes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:16.5334em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_37" style="word-spacing:0.0273em;">U Wave: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">Does not appear in all patients May be caused &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:17.6833em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">by low potassium (hypokalemia) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:15.3879em;top:43.6899em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">Note: Cardioversion vs. Defibrillation &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:46.0541em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Cardioversion</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:47.1942em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Energy</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">: Low energy &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:48.3486em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Condition</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">: Used </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_17">ON</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_41">L</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0008em;">Y </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">if the patient has a pulse &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:49.5029em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Preparation</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">: Premedication with sedation and analgesics &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:50.6573em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Synchronization</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">: Synchronize the machine with the patient's heart rhythm. </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0004em;">SYNC ON. </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">The shock is timed &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:51.8213em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">to hit the start of the </span><span class="pdf24_42 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">R </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:0em;">wave to normalize the heart rate &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:52.9711em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Defibrillation</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:54.1112em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Energy</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_43" style="word-spacing:0.0045em;">: High energy &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:55.2655em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Condition</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">: Used in emergencies for life-threatening arrhythmias such as ventricular fibrillation or </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_31">pulseless &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:56.4296em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">ventricular tachycardia &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:57.5697em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_44">Synchronization</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">: Unsynchronized; the shock is delivered immediately &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_3" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.883em;top:2.5524em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_09" style="word-spacing:0.0126em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 4 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:19.2307em;top:4.3054em;"><a href="https://skillstat.com/wp-content/themes/childTheme/assets/ecg-simulator/html5/index.html" target="_blank"><span class="pdf24_10 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0em;">ECG/EKG Strips &nbsp;</span></a></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:7.4198em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">Sinus Rhythm (Normal) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:18.8969em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">Sinus rhythm is the normal heart rhythm characterized by: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:20.7549em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_48" style="word-spacing:0.0018em;">A regular heart rate of 60-100 bpm &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:22.0054em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">Regular rhythm &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:23.2512em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Consistent P waves preceding each narrow QRS complex &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:24.5017em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">A normal PR interval of 0.12-0.20 seconds &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:25.7521em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A flat ST segment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:27.6384em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">These features indicate proper functioning of the heart's electrical conduction system. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:29.9176em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_50" style="word-spacing:0.0051em;">No Treatment needed &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:33.4678em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0007em;">Depolarization Event: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:35.2787em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_51">T</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">o</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">wards a Lead: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">Results in a bump up on the ECG &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:36.433em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_52" style="word-spacing:0.0185em;">Away from a Lead: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">Results in a dip down on the ECG &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:38.2679em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0007em;">Repolarization Event: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:40.0788em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_51">T</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">o</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">wards a Lead: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">Results in a dip down on the ECG &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:41.2331em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_52" style="word-spacing:0.0185em;">Away from a Lead: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">Results in a bump up on the ECG &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:43.0679em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Depolarization: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:44.8788em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">Sodium (Na+) enters the cell through funny sodium &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:46.0429em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_22" style="word-spacing:0.0006em;">channels, exciting the cell to a positive charge, which &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:47.1928em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0006em;">then opens calcium (Ca2+) channels, allowing calcium &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:48.3427em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">to enter and further depolarize the cell &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:49.4828em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">Occurs spontaneously and spreads through the muscle &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:50.6469em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_48" style="word-spacing:0.0019em;">in a domino effect fashion &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:52.5289em;"><span class="pdf24_53 pdf24_08 pdf24_12">Repolarization: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:54.2758em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_12" style="word-spacing:0.7552em;">● Involves</span><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">&nbsp;</span><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_22" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">the movement of potassium (K+) ions out of &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:55.3298em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_12" style="word-spacing:0em;">the cells &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_4" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.883em;top:2.5524em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_09" style="word-spacing:0.0126em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 5 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.2877em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0006em;">Sinus Bradycardia &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:15.7648em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">Sinus bradycardia is a heart rhythm characterized by: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:17.6228em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_54" style="word-spacing:0.0016em;">A regular heart rate of less than 60 bpm &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:18.8706em;"><span class="pdf24_55 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">Regular rhythm &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:20.0276em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Consistent P waves preceding each narrow QRS complex &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.2781em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">A normal PR interval of 0.12-0.20 seconds &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:22.5286em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A flat ST segment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:24.4149em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">These features indicate a decreased heart rate originating from the sinoatrial (SA) node. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.6366em;top:27.3208em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_56">Treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:10.7631em;top:29.9042em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_17">Symptomatic &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:6.2809em;top:31.15em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_57" style="word-spacing:0.0044em;">(Pale, Dizzy, Fatigue, Confusion) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:33.65em;top:29.9042em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_17">Asymptomatic &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:33.5143em;top:31.15em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">(No symptoms) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:11.9238em;top:33.3296em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_58">Atropine &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:31.9077em;top:33.3296em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">Document the condition &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:11.6096em;top:35.4277em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Monitor &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:10.8178em;top:37.7919em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">Blood pressure &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:11.8466em;top:38.9418em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">Heart rate &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:41.0292em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">Atropine is an </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_59" style="word-spacing:0.0034em;">adrenergic </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0008em;">medication, meaning it can &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:42.1791em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0006em;">activate the sympathetic nervous system, which will &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:43.329em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">increase blood pressure and heart rate. Therefore, when &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:44.4789em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.001em;">administering this medication, </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">it is essential to &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:45.6288em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_48" style="word-spacing:0.0019em;">monitor the patient's blood pressure and heart rate. &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_5" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.883em;top:2.5524em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_09" style="word-spacing:0.0126em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 6 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.2877em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0008em;">Sinus </span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_51">T</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">a</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">chycardia &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:15.7648em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">Sinus tachycardia is a heart rhythm characterized by: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:17.6228em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_48" style="word-spacing:0.0017em;">A regular heart rate of 100-150 bpm &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:18.8733em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">Regular rhythm &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:20.119em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Consistent P waves preceding each narrow QRS complex &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.3695em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">A normal PR interval of 0.12 - 0.20 seconds &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:22.62em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A flat ST segment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:24.5063em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">These features indicate an increased heart rate originating from the sinoatrial (SA) node. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.6679em;top:27.4122em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_56">Treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:29.9957em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Calcium Channel Blockers (CCBs) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.175em;top:31.8509em;z-index:364;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_31" style="word-spacing:-0.0015em;">Examples: Diltiazem and </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_30">V</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_31">e</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16">rapamil &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.175em;top:33.0052em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">Important Note: Do not give these medications to patients with COPD or any respiratory issues &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:35.1103em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">Digoxin &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.175em;top:36.9656em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_60" style="word-spacing:0.0061em;">Digitalis Toxicity Signs: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:7.175em;top:38.1199em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">○ </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">Early Signs: Nausea and vomiting &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:7.175em;top:39.2742em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">○ </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_61" style="word-spacing:0.0027em;">Late Signs: Visual disturbances, such as halos over objects &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.175em;top:40.4285em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">Dehydration: Stop digoxin if there are signs of dehydration (e.g., dry mucous membranes). &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:42.5336em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Beta Blockers (BB) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:44.3986em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">Monitoring Heart Rate (HR): &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.175em;top:46.1137em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_22" style="word-spacing:0.0005em;">Adults: Stop the medication if HR is under 60 bpm (even 59 bpm means STOP). &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.175em;top:47.268em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">Children: Stop the medication if HR is under 70 bpm. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.175em;top:48.4223em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">Infants: Stop the medication if HR is under 90 bpm. &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_6" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.883em;top:2.5524em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_09" style="word-spacing:0.0126em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 7 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.2877em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">Supraventricular </span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_51">T</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">a</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">chycardia SVT &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:15.7648em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) is a heart rhythm characterized by: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:17.6228em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_48" style="word-spacing:0.0018em;">A regular heart rate above 150 bpm &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:18.8733em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">Regular rhythm &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:20.119em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Consistent P waves may be present but often obscured or hidden within the preceding T waves &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.3695em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0em;">Narrow QRS complexes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:22.62em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A normal PR interval if P waves are visible &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:23.8705em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A flat ST segment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:25.7568em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">These features indicate a rapid heart rate </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_20" style="word-spacing:0.001em;">originating above the ventricles, from the atria or the &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:27.0025em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">atrioventricular (</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_62">A</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_17">V</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">) node. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.6679em;top:29.9085em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_56">Treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:32.2664em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">1. </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_51">V</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">a</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">gal (</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_51">V</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">a</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">lsalva) Maneuver </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">- Stimulates the vagus nerve (Cranial Nerve 10) to help manage supraventricular &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:33.4163em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.001em;">tachycardia (SVT) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:35.0356em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">Ice Stimulation</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">: Splash cold water on the patient's face and place ice on the chest &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:36.1899em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">Bearing Down</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">: Have the patient bear down as if having a bowel movement &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:37.3442em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">Blowing into a Straw</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">: Give the patient a narrow straw and ask them to blow into it &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:38.9874em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0004em;">2. Adenosine Push</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.2509em;">&nbsp;</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">- </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0006em;">Administer adenosine by injecting it into the IV and immediately flush with normal saline &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:40.1275em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">IV Channels Setup</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:6.7375em;top:41.2818em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">○ </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">One IV channel for blood tests &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:6.7375em;top:42.4361em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">○ </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">One IV channel for administering adenosine &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:6.7375em;top:43.5904em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">○ </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">One IV channel for flushing with normal saline &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:44.7447em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Monitoring</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">: Monitor for hypotension after administration &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:46.3879em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">3. Cardioversion </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">- Used to restore a normal heart rhythm </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_17">ON</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_63">L</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">Y </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0008em;">in patients with arrhythmias </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">who have a pulse &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:47.8155em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Energy</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_58" style="word-spacing:0.005em;">: Low energy &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:48.9725em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_64">Prepar</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">ation</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">: Premedicate with sedation and analgesics &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:50.223em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_29">Synchronization</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">: Synchronize the machine with the patient's heartbeat. The right pad goes on the &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:51.4841em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_65" style="word-spacing:0.0014em;">right shoulder, and the left pad goes under the heart. Ensure the SYNC is ON. Synchronize to hit &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:52.7298em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">the start of the P wave to normalize the heart rate &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:54.263em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">4. </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Medication - Same as Sinus </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_66">T</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_17">a</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">chycardia (CCB, BB, Digoxin) &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_7" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.883em;top:2.5524em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_09" style="word-spacing:0.0126em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 8 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.2877em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">Atrial Flutter &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:15.7648em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">Atrial flutter is a heart rhythm characterized by: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:17.6228em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_65" style="word-spacing:0.0013em;">A regular or irregular heart rate, typically between 250-350 bpm for the atrial rate &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:18.8733em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_22" style="word-spacing:0.0007em;">Sawtooth-shaped flutter waves (F waves) resembling teeth between the QRS complexes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:20.1344em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">instead of distinct P waves &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.3695em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_20" style="word-spacing:0.0009em;">Dysrhythmia (risk of blood clot formation) (risk of stroke) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:22.62em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0em;">Narrow QRS complexes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:23.8705em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A normal PR interval, although it may be variable due to the rapid atrial rate &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:25.121em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A flat ST segment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:27.0073em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">These features indicate a rapid atrial rhythm caused by a reentrant circuit within the atria. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.6679em;top:29.9132em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_56">Treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:32.7264em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">A. Anticoagulants &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:34.3456em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Purpose</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">: Prevent clot formation. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:35.4999em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Reason</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">: Dysrhythmias can cause blood clots, leading to ischemia and stroke (ischemic stroke). &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:37.1431em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">B. Beta Blockers (BB) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:38.7624em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Purpose</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">: Lower heart rate and manage dysrhythmias. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:40.4056em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">C. Calcium Channel Blockers (CCBs) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:42.0248em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Purpose</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">: Lower heart rate and manage dysrhythmias. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:43.668em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">D. Digoxin &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:45.2873em;z-index:809;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Purpose</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_27" style="word-spacing:0.0047em;">: Lower heart rate and improve heart efficiency. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:46.9305em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_54" style="word-spacing:0.0019em;">E. Electro Cardioversion &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:48.5497em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Purpose</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">: Restore normal heart rhythm &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:50.193em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">So it will be A (an anticoagulant) </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">+ </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0007em;">either of the heart rate-lowering medications </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">+ </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0009em;">electro cardioversion &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:51.8326em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">NOTE &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:53.7455em;"><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_12">Consultation</span><span class="pdf24_68 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0359em;">: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">Always question the physician if two heart rate-lowering medications are prescribed together &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:55.5662em;"><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_12" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Combination Therapy</span><span class="pdf24_68 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0359em;">: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">Use one anticoagulant plus one heart rate-lowering medication or electro &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:56.9007em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0007em;">cardioversion. </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">DO NOT </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">use two anticoagulants or two heart rate-lowering medications together ! &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_8" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.883em;top:2.5524em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_09" style="word-spacing:0.0126em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 9 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.2876em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">Atrial Fibrillation &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:15.7649em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">Atrial fibrillation is a heart rhythm characterized by: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:17.6228em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">An irregular heart rate that can vary from slow to rapid &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:18.8733em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_18" style="word-spacing:0.0013em;">No distinct P waves, with a wavy baseline instead resembling an irregular series of teeth &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:20.1238em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_20" style="word-spacing:0.0009em;">Dysrhythmia (risk of blood clot formation) (risk of stroke) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.3743em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0em;">Narrow QRS complexes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:22.6248em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">An absent or indiscernible PR interval due to the lack of distinct P waves &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:23.8753em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A flat ST segment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:25.1258em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">An abnormal heart sound, not "lub dub," but rather "llllll dub lllll dub" &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:27.0121em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_22" style="word-spacing:0.0005em;">These features indicate a disorganized and rapid atrial rhythm caused by multiple reentrant circuits within &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:28.2578em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">the atria. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.6679em;top:31.1637em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_56">Treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:33.9769em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">A. Anticoagulants &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:35.5961em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Purpose</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">: Prevent clot formation &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:36.7504em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Reason</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">: Dysrhythmias can cause blood clots, leading to ischemia and stroke (ischemic stroke) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:38.3936em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">B. Beta Blockers (BB) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:40.0129em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Purpose</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">: Lower heart rate and manage dysrhythmias &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:41.6561em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">C. Calcium Channel Blockers (CCBs) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:43.2753em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Purpose</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">: Lower heart rate and manage dysrhythmias &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:44.9185em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">D. Digoxin &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:46.5378em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Purpose</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_69" style="word-spacing:0.0008em;">: Lower heart rate and improve heart efficiency &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:48.181em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_54" style="word-spacing:0.0019em;">E. Electro Cardioversion &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:49.8002em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Purpose</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">: Restore normal heart rhythm &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:51.4434em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">So it will be A (an anticoagulant) </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">+ </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0007em;">either of the heart rate-lowering medications </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">+ </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0009em;">electro cardioversion &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:53.0831em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">NOTE &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:54.996em;"><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_12">Consultation</span><span class="pdf24_68 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0359em;">: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">Always question the physician if two heart rate-lowering medications are prescribed together &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:56.8167em;"><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_12" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Combination Therapy</span><span class="pdf24_68 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0359em;">: </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">Use one anticoagulant plus one heart rate-lowering medication or electro &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:58.3524em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0007em;">cardioversion. </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">DO NOT </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">use two anticoagulants or two heart rate-lowering medications together ! &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_9" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.3736em;top:2.5524em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_70" style="word-spacing:0.0117em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 10 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.2876em;z-index:52;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_51">V</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_31">e</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0006em;">ntricular </span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_51">T</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">a</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">chycardia V</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_51">T</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">e</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">ch &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:15.7649em;z-index:56;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_71">V</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">e</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_72" style="word-spacing:0.003em;">ntricular tachycardia (VTech) is a heart rhythm characterized by: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:17.6228em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_48" style="word-spacing:0.0017em;">A regular heart rate of 100-250 bpm &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:18.8733em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">No distinct P waves, with wide and bizarre QRS complexes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:20.1238em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_61" style="word-spacing:0.0025em;">Wide QRS complexes (greater than 0.12 seconds) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.3743em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">An absent or indiscernible PR interval due to the lack of distinct P waves &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:22.6248em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A flat ST segment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:24.5111em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">These features indicate a rapid rhythm originating from the ventricles, often referred to as a "</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">dead strip</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">" &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:25.7568em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">or "</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">dying patient rhythm</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">" due to its life-threatening nature. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.6991em;top:28.6627em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_56">Treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:11.1359em;top:31.2497em;"><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_73" style="word-spacing:0.0053em;">With Pulse &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:33.576em;top:31.2497em;"><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_32" style="word-spacing:0.0039em;">Without Pulse &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:26.4055em;top:33.5217em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">First, call for help (Code Blue), then start CPR (using &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:27.1853em;top:34.6716em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">the rhythm of the song "Stayin' Alive" by the Bee &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:30.3789em;top:35.8215em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">Gees), and perform defibrillation. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:9.2663em;top:34.6716em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">Perform Cardioversion &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.224em;top:37.9195em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Medications &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:12.5893em;top:40.2838em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0008em;">Epinephrine (Adrenaline), Amiodaron, Lidocaine, Procainamide &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:9.0588em;top:41.4337em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">These medications are commonly used in the management of cardiac emergencies &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7419em;top:43.8169em;"><span class="pdf24_74 pdf24_11 pdf24_12" style="word-spacing:-0.0289em;">NOTE: </span><span class="pdf24_75 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">If you suspect that a patient has potential fractures due to a fall or any trauma &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.3674em;top:45.4388em;"><span class="pdf24_75 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">(such as spinal or brain injuries), </span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">DO NOT </span><span class="pdf24_75 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">perform CPR to prevent further damage. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:15.2982em;top:46.8762em;"><span class="pdf24_75 pdf24_11 pdf24_69" style="word-spacing:0.0004em;">Call emergency services immediately !!! &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_10" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.4416em;top:2.5524em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_76" style="word-spacing:0.0231em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 11 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.2876em;z-index:29;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_51">V</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_31">e</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0009em;">ntricular Fibrillation VFib &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:15.7649em;z-index:56;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_71">V</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">e</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">ntricular fibrillation (VFib) is a heart rhythm characterized by: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:17.6228em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_48" style="word-spacing:0.0017em;">An extremely irregular heart rate with no discernible pattern &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:18.8733em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">No distinct P waves, with chaotic and irregular electrical activity &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:20.1238em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">No discernible QRS complexes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.3743em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">An absent or indiscernible PR interval due to the lack of distinct P waves and QRS complexes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:22.6248em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A flat ST segment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:23.8753em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_65" style="word-spacing:0.0015em;">No effective pulse, as this rhythm does not result in effective heartbeats &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:25.7616em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">These features indicate a chaotic rhythm originating from the ventricles, which is immediately &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:27.0073em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">life-threatening. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.6679em;top:29.9132em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_56">Treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:11.1359em;top:32.5002em;"><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_73" style="word-spacing:0.0053em;">With Pulse &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:33.5448em;top:32.5002em;"><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_32" style="word-spacing:0.0039em;">Without Pulse &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:26.3742em;top:34.7721em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">First, call for help (Code Blue), then start CPR (using &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:26.0295em;top:35.922em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">the rhythm of the song "Stayin' Alive" by the Bee Gees &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:29.9582em;top:37.0719em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">30 to 2), and perform defibrillation. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:9.2663em;top:35.922em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">Perform Cardioversion &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.1927em;top:39.17em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Medications &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:12.5581em;top:41.5343em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0008em;">Epinephrine (Adrenaline), Amiodaron, Lidocaine, Procainamide &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:9.0275em;top:42.6842em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">These medications are commonly used in the management of cardiac emergencies &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7419em;top:45.0673em;"><span class="pdf24_74 pdf24_11 pdf24_12" style="word-spacing:-0.0289em;">NOTE: </span><span class="pdf24_75 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">If you suspect that a patient has potential fractures due to a fall or any trauma &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.3674em;top:46.6893em;"><span class="pdf24_75 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">(such as spinal or brain injuries), </span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">DO NOT </span><span class="pdf24_75 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">perform CPR to prevent further damage. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:15.2982em;top:48.1267em;"><span class="pdf24_75 pdf24_11 pdf24_69" style="word-spacing:0.0004em;">Call emergency services immediately !!! &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_11" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.3736em;top:2.5524em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_70" style="word-spacing:0.0117em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 12 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.2876em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_57" style="word-spacing:0.0046em;">NSR with PVC (Normal Sinus Rhythm with Premature Ventricular Contractions) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:15.7613em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_57" style="word-spacing:0.0047em;">Normal Sinus Rhythm with Premature Ventricular Contractions (NSR with PVC) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:17.3805em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_61" style="word-spacing:0.0026em;">A regular heart rate </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0009em;">with occasional irregular beats. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:18.5348em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">Consistent P waves </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">associated with normal QRS complexes during sinus rhythm. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:19.6891em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_59" style="word-spacing:0.004em;">Premature QRS complexes </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">that appear wider and bizarre compared to the normal QRS complexes. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:20.8434em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">T waves </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">may drop from time to time &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.9977em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_72" style="word-spacing:0.0034em;">No preceding P wave </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">before the PVC, as PVCs originate from the ventricles. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:23.152em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">A compensatory pause </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">often follows the PVC, allowing the next normal beat to occur on time. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:24.3063em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">Normal PR interval </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_54" style="word-spacing:0.0016em;">in beats not affected by PVCs. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:25.4606em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">A normal ST segment </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_65" style="word-spacing:0.0014em;">in the beats not affected by PVCs. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:27.1038em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">These features indicate that while the heart maintains a normal rhythm overall, occasional extra beats (PVCs) arise &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:28.2538em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">from the ventricles. PVCs can occur due to stimulants like caffeine or narcotics. It is important to check for &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:29.4037em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_18" style="word-spacing:0.0011em;">stimulants and calm the patient by talking to them. However, if the patient has </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_65" style="word-spacing:0.0014em;">had a myocardial infarction (MI) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:30.5536em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">and </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_72" style="word-spacing:0.0033em;">PVCs are observed </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">in the recovery stage, it may indicate that </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">another MI </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">is </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.001em;">COMING SOON</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">. More than &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:31.7035em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">two PVCs on the strip is considered a high risk of MI. In such cases, the nurse should immediately contact the &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:32.8534em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16">physician. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.6366em;top:35.667em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_56">Treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:38.2468em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_22" style="word-spacing:0.0007em;">The nurse must differentiate why the patient has PVCs. If the patient has consumed caffeine or narcotics, no &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:39.3967em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0008em;">immediate intervention is needed; </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">just focus on calming the heart rate</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_59" style="word-spacing:0.0038em;">. However, if the patient is in the &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:40.5466em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_32" style="word-spacing:0.0041em;">recovery stage from an MI</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0006em;">, immediately contact the physician as </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">another MI may be imminent</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">. &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_12" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.3736em;top:2.5524em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_70" style="word-spacing:0.0117em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 13 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.2876em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">Cardiac </span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_51">T</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">a</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">mponade &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:15.7613em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0006em;">1. Electrical Alternans: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:17.3805em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">This is a pathognomonic feature of cardiac tamponade where there is a beat-to-beat alternation in the QRS &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:18.5446em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_69" style="word-spacing:0.0008em;">complex amplitude or axis due to the heart swinging within the large pericardial effusion. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:20.1737em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">2. Low </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_51">V</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">o</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">ltage QRS: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.7929em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0em;">The QRS complexes may appear smaller (low voltage) due to the dampening effect of the fluid surrounding &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:22.957em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">the heart. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:24.586em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">3. Sinus </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_51">T</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">a</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">chycardia: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:26.2052em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">An increased heart rate is often observed as the heart attempts to compensate for reduced cardiac output. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:27.8485em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_48" style="word-spacing:0.002em;">4. PR Segment Depression: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:29.4677em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">Depression of the PR segment can be seen in the precordial leads, indicative of pericardial inflammation. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:31.1109em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">These EKG findings, particularly in combination, are suggestive of cardiac tamponade and should prompt further &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:32.2608em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.001em;">investigation and immediate medical intervention. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.6366em;top:35.0744em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_56">Treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:37.6543em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">1. Pericardiocentesis: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.175em;top:38.967em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">A procedure to remove excess fluid from the pericardial sac using a needle. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.175em;top:40.2943em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0em;">Has both therapeutic and diagnostic effects. The fluid is cultured to identify bacterial or microbial &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.675em;top:41.6315em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_77" style="word-spacing:0.0019em;">presence and antibiotic sensitivity, guiding appropriate treatment. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:42.9539em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.0003em;">2. Fluid Resuscitation: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.175em;top:44.2665em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">Intravenous fluids to maintain blood pressure and cardiac output. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.175em;top:45.594em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">Hydrate the patient before any fluid aspiration to prevent hypotension. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:46.9312em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_78" style="word-spacing:0.0077em;">3. Treatment of Underlying Cause: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.175em;top:48.2438em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_18" style="word-spacing:0.0013em;">Address the root cause, such as infection, cancer, or autoimmune disease. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:49.5811em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">4. Emergency Action: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.175em;top:50.8937em;z-index:1267;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_77" style="word-spacing:0.0018em;">Since cardiac tamponade is life-threatening, call a physician immediately. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.175em;top:52.221em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0008em;">Cardiac tamponade requires immediate medical attention to prevent severe complications or death. Seek &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.675em;top:53.5583em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_20" style="word-spacing:0.0009em;">emergency medical care if suspected. &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_13" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.3736em;top:2.5524em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_70" style="word-spacing:0.0117em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 14 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.1059em;top:4.2841em;"><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_12" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">Heart block </span><span class="pdf24_68 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">is a condition where the </span><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_12" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">SA node </span><span class="pdf24_68 pdf24_11 pdf24_12" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">and </span><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_79">A</span><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">V </span><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_12">n</span><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">ode fail </span><span class="pdf24_68 pdf24_11 pdf24_80" style="word-spacing:0.0076em;">to work properly, affecting the &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:10.2625em;top:5.8269em;"><span class="pdf24_68 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0em;">heart's </span><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">electrical conduction system</span><span class="pdf24_68 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">. It has </span><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">4 </span><span class="pdf24_68 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">degrees of severity &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:8.6212em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_72" style="word-spacing:0.0033em;">First-Degree </span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_21">A</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">V </span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">B</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_43" style="word-spacing:0.0041em;">lock With NSR &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:20.0984em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">Normal sinus rhythm with first-degree </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_62">A</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">V </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_17">b</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">lock is characterized by: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.9563em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_48" style="word-spacing:0.0018em;">A regular heart rate of 60-100 bpm &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:23.2068em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">Regular rhythm &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:24.4526em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Consistent P waves preceding each narrow QRS complex &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:25.7031em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_29" style="word-spacing:0.0023em;">A prolonged PQ interval &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:26.9536em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">A normal QRS duration &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:28.2041em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A flat ST segment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:30.0903em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">These features indicate a normal heart rhythm with a delay in the conduction between the atria and &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:31.3361em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">ventricles, often caused by </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">factors such as beta blockers or age</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">. </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">This strip is often mistaken for &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:32.5818em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_18" style="word-spacing:0.0013em;">normal sinus rhythm. If beta blockers are stopped, the PQ interval might return to normal. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.6366em;top:35.4878em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_56">Treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:38.0711em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">No treatment is needed; just check the medication list. If there is a CCB or BB, check the heart rate and &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:39.3169em;z-index:760;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_59" style="word-spacing:0.0038em;">consider stopping the medication if necessary. &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_14" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.3736em;top:2.5524em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_70" style="word-spacing:0.0117em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 15 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.2876em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_81" style="word-spacing:0.0031em;">Second-Degree </span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_21">A</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">V </span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">B</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_82" style="word-spacing:0.0111em;">lock Type I (Wenckebach or Mobitz I) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:15.7649em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Second-degree </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_62">A</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">V </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">b</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_43" style="word-spacing:0.0043em;">lock type I (Wenckebach or Mobitz I) is characterized by: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:17.6228em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">A regular or irregular heart rate, typically within the normal range &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:18.8733em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Consistent P waves with a progressively lengthening PR interval until a </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">1 QRS complex is &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:20.1344em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_83">dropped &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.3696em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">A normal QRS duration &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:22.6201em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Characterized by one straight line in between normal QRS complexes due to gradual P wave &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:23.8812em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">lengthening &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:25.1163em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A flat ST segment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:27.0026em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">These features indicate a progressive delay in the conduction between the atria and ventricles until a beat &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:28.2483em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">is dropped, often caused by factors such as increased vagal tone or medications. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.6366em;top:31.1542em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_56">Treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:33.7341em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">Pacemaker is needed</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">. No other special treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_15" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.3736em;top:2.5524em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_70" style="word-spacing:0.0117em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 16 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.2876em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_81" style="word-spacing:0.0031em;">Second-degree </span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_21">A</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">V </span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_17">b</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">lock type II (Mobitz II) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:15.7649em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Second-degree </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_62">A</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">V </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">b</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">lock type II (Mobitz II) is characterized by: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:17.6228em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">A regular or irregular heart rate, </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">often slower than normal (Bradycardia) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:18.8733em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Consistent P waves </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">with a constant PR interval for conducted beats, but occasional </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">P waves that &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:20.1344em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_69" style="word-spacing:0.0008em;">do not lead to QRS complexes (non-conducted P waves). Usually 2 or more P waves. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.3696em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">A normal QRS duration &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:22.6201em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A flat ST segment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:23.8706em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Patients typically experience bradycardia</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">, with a heart rate often ranging between </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">30-50 </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">beats &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:25.1316em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">per minute &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:26.3667em;z-index:499;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Multiple straight lines indicate a need for a pacemake</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_84">r</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_84">. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:28.2531em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">These features indicate an intermittent failure of the conduction between the atria and ventricles without &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:29.4988em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">progressive PR interval prolongation, often caused by structural heart disease or significant conduction &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:30.7445em;z-index:743;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_44" style="word-spacing:0.0023em;">system disease. This condition requires the placement of a pacemaker. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.6366em;top:33.6504em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_56">Treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:36.2302em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0008em;">Pacemaker is needed. </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">No other special treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_16" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.3736em;top:2.5523em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_70" style="word-spacing:0.0117em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 17 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.2877em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_72" style="word-spacing:0.0033em;">Third-Degree </span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_21">A</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">V </span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">B</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">lock (Complete Heart Block) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:15.7648em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Third-degree </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_62">A</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">V </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">b</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">lock (complete heart block) is characterized by: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:17.6228em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A regular or irregular heart rate, often significantly slower than normal &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:18.8733em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">P waves that occur independently of the QRS complexes, indicating </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_77" style="word-spacing:0.002em;">no relationship between &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:20.1344em;z-index:326;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">atrial and ventricular contractions (no communication between Atria and </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_66">V</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">e</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">ntricles) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.3696em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">A normal or widened QRS duration, depending on the location of the block within the conduction &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:22.6307em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">system &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:23.8658em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A flat ST segment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:25.1163em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Patients typically experience a heart rate often ranging between 30-40 beats per minute &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:27.0026em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">These features indicate a complete failure of the conduction between the atria and ventricles, often caused &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:28.2483em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">by significant structural heart disease or severe conduction system disease. </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_32" style="word-spacing:0.0041em;">This condition requires &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:29.494em;z-index:768;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_18" style="word-spacing:0.0013em;">immediate medical attention, and a nurse should prepare for the placement of a pacemake</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_84">r</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_84">. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.6366em;top:32.3999em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_56">Treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.675em;top:34.9797em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0008em;">Pacemaker is needed. </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">No other special treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_17" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.3736em;top:2.5523em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_70" style="word-spacing:0.0117em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 18 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.2877em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0007em;">Artificial Pacemakers ECG &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:15.7648em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">An EKG with an artificial pacemaker is characterized by: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:17.4763em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0006em;">A regular heart rate, typically programmed by the pacemaker &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:18.6306em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">Regular rhythm dictated by the pacemaker &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:19.7849em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">Presence of pacing spikes (small vertical lines) before P waves or QRS complexes, depending on the type of &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:20.949em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16">pacemaker &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:22.0891em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">Consistent P waves and/or QRS complexes following the pacing spikes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:23.2434em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">Normal or widened QRS duration, depending on the location of pacing (atrial or ventricular) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:24.3977em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">A normal ST segment if no underlying ischemia or other abnormalities are present &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:26.0444em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_18" style="word-spacing:0.0011em;">These features indicate a heart rhythm maintained by an artificial pacemaker, ensuring proper heart rate &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:27.2902em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">and rhythm in patients with bradycardia or other arrhythmias. The pacing spikes are key indicators of &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:28.5359em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">pacemaker activity on the EKG strip. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:30.2607em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Atrial Pacing &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:31.9723em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Purpose</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">: When the SA node in the heart fails &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:33.1266em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_61" style="word-spacing:0.0026em;">EKG strip Feature</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">: A spike preceding the P wave &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:34.7733em;z-index:787;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_66">V</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">e</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">ntricular Pacing &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:36.4849em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Purpose</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">: When a patient has a complete </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_21">A</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">V </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">b</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">lock (e.g., a third-degree </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_21">A</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">V </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">b</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16">lock) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:37.6392em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_61" style="word-spacing:0.0026em;">EKG strip Feature</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">: A spike prior to the QRS complex &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:39.2859em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_62" style="word-spacing:0.258em;">AV </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_17">P</span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">acing &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:40.9974em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Purpose</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">: When a patient has both SA node failure and a complete </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_21">A</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">V </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">b</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16">lock &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:42.1517em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_29" style="word-spacing:0.0024em;">EKG strip Features</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_29" style="word-spacing:0.0024em;">: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:6.7375em;top:43.306em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">○ </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">A spike preceding the P wave (atrial pacing) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:6.7375em;top:44.4603em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">○ </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">Another spike prior to the QRS complex (ventricular pacing) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:46.1035em;z-index:1178;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_18" style="word-spacing:0.0012em;">These features on an EKG strip help identify the type of pacing a patient is receiving from their pacemaker. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:18.5181em;top:48.2432em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_32" style="word-spacing:0.0042em;">NOTE: Failure to capture &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:4.5511em;top:50.6145em;"><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_12" style="word-spacing:0.0002em;">When we see a missing QRS complex after the pacemaker spike, it indicates that the &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:15.4284em;top:51.9561em;"><span class="pdf24_67 pdf24_11 pdf24_54" style="word-spacing:0.0014em;">pacemaker battery needs to be replaced. &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_18" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.3736em;top:2.5523em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_70" style="word-spacing:0.0117em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 19 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.2877em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Asystole &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:15.7648em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">Asystole is characterized by: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:17.6228em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A complete absence of electrical activity in the heart, resulting in a flat line on the EKG &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:18.8733em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">No discernible P waves, QRS complexes, or T waves &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:20.1238em;"><span class="pdf24_46 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_77" style="word-spacing:0.002em;">No heart rate or pulse present &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.3743em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A flat ST segment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:22.6248em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">Often referred to as biological death, indicating the complete and irreversible cessation of all vital &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:23.8859em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">functions after approximately 5 minutes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:25.6107em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_48" style="word-spacing:0.0018em;">These features indicate a total cessation of cardiac electrical activity, often caused by severe cardiac or &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:26.8564em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">systemic conditions. This condition requires immediate medical intervention, including cardiopulmonary &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:28.1022em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">resuscitation (CPR) and advanced cardiac life support (ACLS) protocols. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.6366em;top:31.008em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_56">Treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:33.3696em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Check Heart Rate (HR) and Pulse: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:35.0945em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.385em;">1. First,</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">&nbsp;</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">check the patient's pulse &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:36.3402em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.3851em;">2. Then,</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">&nbsp;</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">check the machine for confirmation &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:38.0651em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">If the Patient Has No Pulse: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:39.7899em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.385em;">3. Call</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">&nbsp;</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">for help (Code Blue) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:41.0356em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.385em;">4. Start</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0em;">&nbsp;</span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">CPR (use the rhythm of the song "Stayin' Alive" by the Bee Gees) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:42.7605em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40">Medications: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:44.4747em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">Administer Epinephrine (Adrenaline) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:16.8311em;top:46.7362em;"><span class="pdf24_85 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">NOTE (</span><span class="pdf24_74 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0em;">NCLEX guidelines) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:13.1875em;top:49.4873em;"><span class="pdf24_74 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0em;">Never perform defibrillation for asystole &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div id="page_19" class="pdf24_ pdf24_02">
			<div class="pdf24_03">
				<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="" class="pdf24_04" />
			</div>
			<div class="pdf24_view">
				<div class="pdf24_05 pdf24_06">
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:40.3736em;top:2.5523em;"><span class="pdf24_07 pdf24_08 pdf24_70" style="word-spacing:0.0117em;">Hrach Vardanyan | 20 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:4.2877em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_51">T</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">o</span><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0001em;">rsades de Pointes &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:15.7648em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_27" style="word-spacing:0.0049em;">Torsades de Pointes is characterized by: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:17.6228em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_18" style="word-spacing:0.0013em;">A distinctive, irregular, and rapid QRS complex pattern on the EKG, which appears to twist around &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:18.8839em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">the baseline &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:20.1191em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_59" style="word-spacing:0.0037em;">Prolonged QT interval preceding the onset of Torsades &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:21.3696em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">No consistent P waves &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:22.6201em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">A highly variable heart rate, often exceeding 200 beats per minute &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:23.8706em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">Often associated with electrolyte imbalances, particularly hypomagnesemia &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:25.1211em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">Can lead to sudden cardiac death if not promptly treated &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:26.3715em;"><span class="pdf24_49 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.5028em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_69" style="word-spacing:0.0007em;">Often referred to as a medical emergency requiring immediate intervention &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:28.2578em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">These features indicate a specific type of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia that can degenerate into &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:29.5035em;"><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">ventricular fibrillation. </span><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_65" style="word-spacing:0.0014em;">This condition requires immediate medical intervention, including the &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:30.7493em;"><span class="pdf24_47 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0em;">administration of magnesium sulfate </span><span class="pdf24_45 pdf24_11 pdf24_40" style="word-spacing:0.0001em;">and advanced cardiac life support (ACLS) protocols. &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:22.6366em;top:33.6552em;"><span class="pdf24_39 pdf24_11 pdf24_56">Treatment &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:36.0132em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Medication</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:37.6325em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">Administer Magnesium Sulfate to sedate the patient &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:39.2756em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">Monitoring</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:40.8949em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">Monitor for signs of magnesium toxicity &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:42.0492em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">Excessive magnesium can lead to </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_32" style="word-spacing:0.0043em;">respiratory distress </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0004em;">due to overly relaxed muscles, </span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_17" style="word-spacing:0.0019em;">including the &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:43.2132em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">diaphragm</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">, causing breathing problems (respiratory distress, muscle weakness, loss of deep tendon &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:5.2375em;top:44.3631em;"><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0007em;">reflexes, hypotension, bradycardia, cardiac arrest) &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:45.9922em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">Therapeutic Range</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:-0.0002em;">: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:47.6115em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0003em;">The therapeutic range for magnesium is 4.5 to 6 &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:2.2375em;top:49.2546em;"><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_51">T</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">o</span><span class="pdf24_13 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">xicity</span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_14">: &nbsp;</span></div>
					<div class="pdf24_01" style="left:3.7375em;top:50.8739em;"><span class="pdf24_24 pdf24_25 pdf24_14" style="word-spacing:0.6182em;">● </span><span class="pdf24_15 pdf24_11 pdf24_16" style="word-spacing:-0.0005em;">Magnesium levels 7 and above indicate toxicity &nbsp;</span></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</body>
</html>
